@meta
id: 08-11
title: Counting lines into the verb
chapter: 08
type: lesson
skills: delete-d
par: 18
minutes: 4
vscode: click the first line, Shift-click the last, Delete
open: order.provider.ts

@brief
A block of debug logging and a method nobody calls have both survived
code review. Between them that is eleven lines to remove.

Pressing `dd` eleven times works and is a waste. A count in front of a
verb multiplies it, exactly as it did with `x`, so a run of adjacent
lines is one command — and one `u` if you get the number wrong.

Take out the debug block, including the blank line that followed it, and
the whole `legacyRefund` method, including the blank line above it.
Nothing else changes.

Turn on `:set relativenumber` if you have not already. It exists for this.

@keys
| `5dd` | delete five lines from the cursor down |
| `7j` | seven lines down |
| `u` | undo, if the count was wrong |

@hints
Two deletions, not eleven. Count the lines you want *including* the blank
one that goes with each block.
--
The count goes in front: `5dd` deletes the current line and the four
below it. With `relativenumber` on, the number is sitting in the margin
next to the last line you want gone.
--
`7j` `5dd` takes the comment, the three logs and the blank line after
them. From there `5j` lands on the blank line above `legacyRefund`, and
`6dd` takes it and the method.

@solution
    7j 5dd  5j 6dd  :wq<Enter>

Ten keystrokes for eleven lines.

The count is doing two jobs. It is faster, obviously. More importantly it
makes the deletion one unit: one entry in the undo history, one thing in
the register, one thing to put back somewhere else with `p` if it turns
out the method was wanted after all.

Counting lines by eye is the weak part, which is why `relativenumber` is
worth turning on — the number you need is printed next to the line you
are looking at. Chapter 9 gives you a better answer still: `d}` deletes
to the end of the paragraph and needs no number at all.

@start order.provider.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    // debug: remove before merge
    console.log('order', order.id);
    console.log('status', order.status);
    console.log('total', order.total);

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'CANCELLED' },
    });
  }

  public async legacyRefund(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    return this.paymentService.refundLegacy(order.id, order.total);
  }
}

@expected order.provider.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'CANCELLED' },
    });
  }
}
